The catch
USA Today headlined the release “September jobs report shows slowdown in hiring, unemployment ticks up”.
25
The bureau says an over-the-month employment change of about 122,000 is statistically significant in the establishment survey.
1
September's estimate of 29,000 is under that bar, and the bureau's own summary is that payrolls “changed little”.
1
The 29,000 is a net change in payroll jobs; hires are a separate count of “all additions to the payroll” during the month, and September's has not been published.
8
By the household survey's estimates, employment rose by 406,000, under that survey's bar of about 650,000 for an employment change, and the labor force rose by 485,000.
1
The estimated labor force grew by more than estimated employment, which is why the count of unemployed rose by 78,000 and the rate went from 4.1 to 4.2 percent.
1
A St. Louis Fed note published October 2 gives the rate without rounding: it “increased slightly from 4.141% to 4.175%”, so most of the visible tenth is rounding.
17
The national rate moved a tenth of a point while the rate for Black workers rose a full point in one month, to 7 percent.
1
A year earlier the rate was 7.6 percent.
From weekly payroll data, ADP Research says private-sector employment increased by 90,000 jobs in September, separate from the bureau's 46,000.
